<div class="page-section mt-4">
    <div class="prose">
        <p class="lead">
            The Actualization Theory did not arrive as a finished axiomatic system. It was assembled, and repeatedly
            stripped down, over a sequence of experiments, audits, and hostile reviews. This page tells that
            story in order.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">1.</span>The predecessor: TRM</h2>
        <p>
            AT's immediate predecessor was the <em>Temporal Resonance Model</em> (TRM), a framework built on
            phase-lattice machinery. TRM had already assembled a striking collection of pieces: a Time Field
            that identified gravity with the phase gradient of a temporal field; a Radial Acceleration
            Relation; Frame Dragging; a Memory Channel; a Theta Chain; an <em>m = 3</em> Closure mechanism; a
            Quantum Engine; a tired-light Temporal Drift; and a Unified Action roadmap.
        </p>
        <p>
            TRM supplied the conceptual vocabulary — a temporal field, oscillation, phase, resonance — on which
            the later theory would be rebuilt. But TRM was a collection of ideas, not yet a theory: it had never
            been systematically audited against a hostile standard.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">2.</span>Problems found: the legacy audit</h2>
        <p>
            The decisive turn was the <em>TRM Legacy Final Classification</em>, a systematic audit that asked
            what, if anything, of the older framework should migrate into a new one. The answer was blunt: TRM
            carried <strong>zero candidate physics</strong> — no module supplied a genuinely new testable
            observable.
        </p>
        <p>
            Of the nine modules, three were absorbed (the Time Field became phase-gradient gravity; the RAR
            became the zero-parameter relation g† = cH0/2π; Frame Dragging was recognized as GR
            gravitomagnetism). Two were rejected outright: the tired-light Temporal Drift was falsified, and the
            Quantum Engine proved non-unitary. Three survived only as candidate mathematics. The residue of
            value was methodological.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">3.</span>ResearchX: alternative foundations</h2>
        <p>
            A parallel track, ResearchX, attacked the foundations from the side. It found eleven hidden
            assumptions — the static graph chief among them — and then tested them one by one. Dynamic graphs
            produced no open-ended innovation. Nonlinearity broke the eigenmode picture, showing that the early
            "quantum" results were linear-algebra artifacts.
        </p>
        <p>
            Out of this stripping emerged the two deep results that anchor the theory: <em>reversibility</em>
            plus <em>self-consistency</em> is the minimally sufficient pair whose intersection is unitary quantum
            mechanics, and quantum reality is the unique global maximum of finite complexity — a necessity, not
            an anthropic accident.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">4.</span>The structure/content split</h2>
        <p>
            The organizing principle of AT was not chosen in advance; it was discovered. An audit asked a
            blunt question: given the primitives, what can actually be <em>computed</em>, and what merely
            <em>is</em>? The answer divided cleanly: everything derivable was form, symmetry, or topology;
            everything that resisted derivation was a dimensionless pure number.
        </p>
        <blockquote>Structure is derivable. Content is realized.</blockquote>
        <p>
            Since no combination of the dimensionful triple (ℓ, τ, ℏ) can produce a dimensionless number, the
            couplings cannot be assembled from the primitives. That turned a limitation into a principle, and
            produced the three-category taxonomy:
            <span class="badge badge-derived">DERIVED</span>
            <span class="badge badge-real">REAL-UNDERIVED</span>
            <span class="badge badge-drawn">DRAWN</span>.
            The ResearchY audits of V2.1/V2.2 later refined this vocabulary into the classification used
            throughout the atlas today:
            <span class="badge badge-derived">DERIVED</span>
            <span class="badge badge-emergent">EMERGENT</span>
            <span class="badge badge-boundary">BOUNDARY</span>.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">5.</span>Hostile reviews</h2>
        <p>
            The theory was subjected to two rounds of hostile review. The first found one valid objection,
            eleven partially valid, and zero invalid — a distribution that pointed at presentation, not at a
            wrong core. The second round raised twelve "FATAL" issues; each was re-evaluated against the
            executable tests.
        </p>
        <p>
            What changed was framing, not physics: "closed" became "dispositioned", "no-go" became "conditional
            no-go", and "derivation" became "ontological reinterpretation". The tally — 6 resolved, 6 partially
            resolved, 0 open — is a record of overstatements downgraded, not of results retracted.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">6.</span>AT v1.0</h2>
        <p>
            The compressed endpoint of the v1.0 era was the theory described throughout the early atlas: two
            irreducible primitives (the individuation principle Q and genuine randomness) plus one contingent
            number (M²). The Schrödinger dynamical core was executable and tested; the Einstein recovery
            remained logical, not mathematical. The theory was ready as a foundation monograph and a
            research-program paper — and honestly not yet as a derivation paper.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">7.</span>The D96 program</h2>
        <p>
            The v1.0 monograph settled what the primitives are. What it did not yet do was turn those
            primitives into numbers that match the measured world. That was the work of the D96 program, a
            deliberate shift from foundational research to quantitative physics. The move was not a change of
            theory — no primitive was added — but a change of target: instead of asking what must exist, the
            program asked what the existing structure actually predicts.
        </p>
        <p>
            The pivot point was a concrete object: the D96 spectral geometry, a period-3 network whose
            spectrum supplies the framework's numerical content. Once D96 was selected (QG128), a chain of
            quantitative results followed in order. The gauge sector was read off the spectrum — couplings
            like 1/α_em = 137 and sin²θ_W = 0.2316 came from the same structure (QG162). The mixing matrices
            followed: CKM (QG165) and PMNS (QG167), each a set of ratios of spectral quantities. The weak boson
            masses MW and MZ were anchored to the calibration scale (QG168), and the Higgs mass MH emerged at
            125.25 GeV with a 0.003% deviation (QG169).
        </p>
        <p>
            The program then pushed deeper into quantities that had never been derived before: neutrino mass
            splittings (QG172), all six absolute quark masses from a single electron anchor (QG173), and the
            strong CP parameter θ_QCD = 0 from a reflection symmetry of the graph (QG174). Precision
            electroweak observables — ΓZ, ΓW, ΓH, Rb, AFB — closed out the standard-model layer (QG175), and
            the Newton constant G was brought into the same scheme (QG181). What had been a foundation program
            became a factory of tested numbers, each with a cited phase and a measured comparison.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">8.</span>Blind validation</h2>
        <p>
            A factory of numbers is only convincing if the numbers are not read back from their targets. That
            is the reason the D96 program's strongest results were produced blind. In QG176, the Higgs was
            treated as unknown: its mass, width, and couplings were hidden, and MH was reconstructed from the
            remaining pre-Higgs D96 quantities alone. The hidden value came back at 125.25 GeV against a
            measured 125.25 GeV — a 0.003% deviation, with no Higgs input anywhere in the computation.
        </p>
        <p>
            QG177 generalized this into a leave-one-out validation. Each of 12 observables was hidden in turn
            and rebuilt from the remaining primitive base. The result was a mean deviation of 0.58% across the
            twelve, with a maximum of 1.89%, and no observable requiring itself as an input.
        </p>
        <p>
            The reason blind reconstruction is stronger than post-hoc fitting is structural. A formula fitted
            to a target reproduces that target by construction — the fit carries the target inside its own
            inputs. A blind reconstruction does not: the target is excluded from the computation and only
            compared afterward. Agreement then measures genuine predictive content, not interpolation. Nine of
            the twelve leave-one-out chains were fully independent reconstructions; the remaining three used
            primitive-inlined canonical sequences, which is weaker but still target-free.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">9.</span>Gravity consolidation</h2>
        <p>
            The gravity side of the program consolidated around a single theme: the same D96 quantities that
            produced the standard model also produce Einstein structure. The Newton constant was fixed from
            the D96 scale (QG181), and the Einstein tensor was recovered from the density field (G4-G2/G3).
            The mass-radius relation M ∝ R was derived from the per-octave deficit profile (QG184), which also
            restored the black-hole temperature law T ∝ 1/R (QG185). The older gravity results were then
            audited and, in most cases, resolved: the perihelion, Newton-G, and Hawking-temperature
            contradictions were examined (QG188A-era audits) and closed.
        </p>
        <p>
            Two further derivations completed the observational layer. Frame dragging was shown to be a
            ψ-sector observable — the gravitomagnetic h₀ᵢ field is zero in the conformal ρ-only sector and
            restored by the ψ tensor field, matching Gravity Probe B (QG186). The GPS correction followed from
            the same redshift law as gravitational time dilation: the net offset of +38.5 μs/day against the
            observed +38.6 μs/day (QG187).
        </p>
        <p>
            The foundational question — what sources the geometry — was resolved by two linked results.
            Matter was derived as the actualization deficit ρ̄ − ρ (QG194), and the matter sector was shown
            to be an independent deficit-dust tensor T_μν = (ρ̄−ρ)·v_μ·v_ν, escaping the Lovelock obstruction
            that would otherwise force every tensor to be proportional to G (QG195). Finally, the native
            program's origin in two dimensions was bridged to d≥3 gravity: the conformal ansatz is
            dimension-generic, and the (d−2) factor connects the 2D degeneracy (G ≡ 0) to the non-trivial 3D
            Einstein structure (QG197). The old gravity contradictions — lensing, Hawking temperature, Newton
            G — were audited and mostly resolved (QG188-era audits), leaving a consolidated and internally
            consistent gravitational layer.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">10.</span>Prediction registry</h2>
        <p>
            The quantitative program ended where a quantitative program must: in predictions that are fixed
            before the data arrives. In QG190–QG192 the three targets were pre-registered, with the values
            frozen from D96 geometry alone and collider data explicitly excluded from the computation. The
            registry itself was locked in QG193, under a rule that no future phase may modify a registered
            prediction.
        </p>
        <p>
            Three predictions are on record. P1 is a resonance at 106.39 GeV, with a search window of
            99–114 GeV. P2 is the neutrinoless double-beta decay mass m_ββ = 2.02 meV. P3 is the sector-ladder
            spectrum — nine resonances from 106.39 to 263.43 GeV, spaced by the unit quantum. Each prediction
            carries its formula, its inputs, its uncertainty, and an explicit falsification condition.
        </p>
        <blockquote>Predictions are immutable. Future data may only mark them.</blockquote>
        <p>
            The states are fixed and few: PENDING, SUPPORTED, CONFIRMED, DISFAVORED, FALSIFIED. A prediction
            can only advance forward, and its frozen value can never be edited. The registry exists so that
            future data either confirms what was claimed, or falsifies it — it cannot be silently adjusted to
            fit.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">11.</span>The V2.0–V2.2 programs</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>V2.0</strong> consolidated the theory into the monograph <em>The Actualization Theory — A
            Reconstruction of Physics from Difference, Actualization and Spectrum</em>: the canonical chain
            Difference → Actualization → Spectrum → Physics, with quantum mechanics, the Standard Model, and
            general relativity hosted as derived layers. QG223 closed the quantum-gravity loop; QG225 verified
            the derivation dependency graph is acyclic. The prediction registry, locked in QG193, carried the
            three pre-registered targets into publication.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>V2.1</strong> was the origin program — the ResearchY audits that asked where each V2.0
            object came from. It derived N = 96 from the observable sector (the zero-defect octave rung), the
            Z2-paired complex sector, reciprocity, the complex state ψ = |ψ|e^{iθ}, and the su(2) compact-form;
            then reduced the theory to a five-item irreducible boundary set (R_001): {Difference, η},
            {Z2-paired sector}, {3 octave families}, {SU(2) gauge + j = 1/2}, {v, m_e}. Thirteen historical
            boundary items were reclassified, and eight structurally new predictions were added (D_046).
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>V2.2</strong> added three programs. The measurement program (M_001–M_010) showed that
            measurement is the actualization readout of both quadratures — reveal and redistribute, never
            create — with the discrete tick (AT-P042) as the one genuinely new prediction relative to QM. The
            new-physics program (NP) found exactly one controllable lever (the phase), made synchronization
            optional, and identified the O(2) exact doublet degeneracy as the strongest D96 prediction. The
            geometry-bridge group (QG_001–QG_011) completed the architecture: geometry and information are two
            faces of the <em>same</em> count density ρ — the measure-preserving conformal metric on one side,
            the KL divergence to uniform on the other, feeding ΩΛ = I_occ/ln K = 0.6839. The S_001 synthesis
            then reconstructed the whole theory as one object, ρ.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">12.</span>Current status</h2>
        <p>
            The project now stands at more than 330 research phases (coverage dashboard) and more than 630
            executable ResearchY tests. It has produced blind reconstructions (QG176/QG177), a methodology
            audit of the whole derivation record (QG189), an immutable prediction registry (QG193), and a
            validation portal presenting all of it in one place. The status of the three registered
            predictions, as of the current outcome dashboard, is:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>P1</strong> (106.39 GeV resonance) — <strong>PENDING</strong>: the 99–114 GeV window is
            neither confirmed nor excluded by current searches (QG199).</li>
            <li><strong>P2</strong> (m_ββ = 2.02 meV) — <strong>PENDING</strong>: no experiment has reached the
            2.02 meV sensitivity (QG191).</li>
            <li><strong>P3</strong> (sector-ladder spectrum) — <strong>SUPPORTED</strong>: the 151.98 GeV rung
            aligns with the combined ~152 GeV diphoton excess, a moderate-significance alignment (QG200/QG201).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            It should be stated plainly: AT is no longer primarily a derivation project. The derivation layer
            is largely complete — the standard model quantities, the gravitational structure, the foundational
            origins, and the V2.2 information–geometry bridge have been produced, audited, and consolidated
            into one architecture. What remains open is not a missing derivation but a missing outcome. The
            future progress of the project depends on experimental results: whether the 106 GeV window fills,
            whether the 0νββ rate appears at the frozen value, and whether the ladder rungs are confirmed or
            excluded. The predictions are on record, and the experiments decide.
        </p>

